On the [[review aggregator]] website [[Rotten Tomatoes]], 93% of 91 critics' reviews for the first season are positive. The website's consensus reads: "John Cena's still in solid form as Peacemaker, leading a bloody good time that gives writer-director James Gunn full permission to let his freak flag fly."<ref name="RTS1">{{Cite Rotten Tomatoes |id=peacemaker_2022 |type=tv |season=1 |title=Peacemaker |access-date=July 24, 2025}}{{cbignore}}</ref> [[Metacritic]], which uses a [[weighted average]], assigned the season a score of 70 out of 100, based on 26 critics, indicating "generally favorable" reviews.<ref name="MCS1">{{Cite Metacritic |id=peacemaker |type=tv |season=1 |title=Peacemaker |access-date=July 24, 2025}}{{cbignore}}</ref> The title sequence was praised,<ref name="TitleSequenceGizmodo" /><ref name="TitleSequenceWrap" /><ref name="TitleSequenceDoG" /> with critics also highlighting Gunn's sense of fun and "raunchy humor", and Cena's performance. Some found other characters and story elements to be underdeveloped.<ref name="RTReviewRoundup" />

On Rotten Tomatoes, 94% of 149 critics' reviews for the second season are positive. The website's consensus reads: "''Peacemaker''{{'}}s second season goes multidimensional while still maintaining a singular focus on emotional stakes, seamlessly transporting this outrageous antihero into a fresh cinematic universe."<ref name="RTS2">{{Cite Rotten Tomatoes |id=peacemaker_2022 |type=tv |season=2 |title=Peacemaker |access-date=September 6, 2026}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Metacritic assigned the season a score of 78 out of 100, based on 18 critics, indicating "generally favorable" reviews.<ref name="MCS2">{{Cite Metacritic |id=peacemaker |type=tv |season=2 |title=Peacemaker |access-date=September 29, 2025}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Critics found the season to be better than the first, calling it "just as dark, brutal, and silly" but more grounded, character focused, and heartwarming, with praise for Cena's performance and the wider cast.<ref name="RTReviewRoundupS2" />

===Accolades===
The first season was nominated for a [[74th Primetime Creative Arts Emmy Awards|Primetime Creative Arts Emmy Award]],<ref name="EmmyAwards" /> an [[50th Annie Awards|Annie Award]],<ref name="Annie2023" /> three [[3rd Critics' Choice Super Awards|Critics' Choice Super Awards]],<ref name="CCSA" /> two [[2022 MTV Movie & TV Awards|MTV Movie & TV Awards]],<ref name="MTVAwards" /> and two [[47th Saturn Awards|Saturn Awards]].<ref name="SaturnAwards" /> The second season was nominated for a [[31st Critics' Choice Awards|Critics' Choice Award]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-12-05 |title=Critics Choice Awards 2026: Full list of film and TV nominees |url=https://www.the-independent.com/arts-entertainment/films/news/critics-choice-awards-nominees-2026-b2879063.html |access-date=2026-01-08 |website=The Independent |language=en}}</ref> an [[4th Astra Creative Arts Awards|Astra Creative Arts Award]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Davidson |first=Denton |date=2025-12-12 |title='Sinners' and 'Frankenstein' top Astra Creative Arts Awards: Full winners list |url=https://www.goldderby.com/film/2025/astra-creative-arts-2025-winners-sinners-frankenstein/ |access-date=2026-01-08 |website=Gold Derby |language=en-US}}</ref> three [[53rd Saturn Awards|Saturn Awards]],<ref>{{Cite web|title='Sinners', 'Avatar: Fire And Ash', 'Fantastic Four' And 'Dexter: Resurrection' Lead Saturn Awards Nominations|url=https://deadline.com/2026/01/2026-saturn-awards-nominations-1236697753/|website=Deadline|date=2026-01-27|access-date=2026-01-28|language=en-US|first=Patrick|last=Hipes}}</ref> and a [[Primetime Emmy Awards|Primetime Emmy]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Outstanding Stunt Coordination For Comedy Programming 2026 - Nominees & Winners Emmy Awards |url=https://www.televisionacademy.com/awards/nominees-winners/2026/outstanding-stunt-coordination-for-comedy-programming |access-date=2026-07-09 |website=Television Academy |language=en}}</ref>

==Podcasts==
HBO Max and DC worked with production company [[Rooster Teeth]] to produce an official, [[aftershow]]-style video podcast titled ''Podly''. Hosted by Fiona Nova and Ify Nwadiwe, each episode includes a recap of one of the first season's episodes as well as interviews with cast and crew. The video podcast was made available to stream on HBO Max and [[YouTube]], with an audio-only version available via audio platforms.<ref name="Podly" />

Gunn announced ''[[Peacemaker: The Official Podcast with James Gunn]]'' in July 2025, a new companion podcast for the series featuring himself and the cast. It begins with a rewatch of the first season, including discussion of what parts of the first season are DCU canon, followed by a new podcast episode after each of the second season's episodes. The podcast is being made available on HBO Max, YouTube, and audio platforms.<ref name="OfficialPodcast" />

==Spin-offs==
In January 2021, [[James Gunn]] said he had ideas for more ''The Suicide Squad'' television spin-offs beyond ''Peacemaker'',<ref name="GunnMoreSpinOffs" /> and confirmed that he was working on another one a year later.<ref name="GunnSecondSpinOff" /> In May 2022, a series featuring Amanda Waller was revealed, with [[Viola Davis]] in negotiations to reprise her DCEU role. Christal Henry was writing the series, which was expected to build off Waller's guest appearances in ''Peacemaker''.<ref name="DavisHenrySpinOff" /> The next month, Gunn said the series that he had revealed earlier was separate from a potential Amanda Waller project though there would be "some blending" of characters from ''Peacemaker'' in both.<ref name="GunnJun2022" /> On January 31, Gunn and Safran unveiled the first projects from their DCU slate, which begins with Chapter One: Gods and Monsters. The second project in the slate was ''[[Waller (TV series)|Waller]]'', with Davis confirmed to be reprising her role. Henry was revealed to be working on the overall story for the DCU with Gunn and some other writers, and was set as co-showrunner of ''Waller'' alongside [[Jeremy Carver]].<ref name="DCUSlateVariety" /> The spin-off is expected to be made after the second season of ''Peacemaker''.<ref name="GunnMar2024" />

In 2024, a tie-in non-canonical<ref>{{Cite web |last=Comments |first=Ray Flook {{!}} |date=2024-10-21 |title=DC Studios: New DCU Not Getting In-Canon Comics "At the Current Time" |url=https://bleedingcool.com/tv/dc-studios-new-dcu-not-getting-in-canon-comics-at-the-current-time/ |access-date=2025-10-10 |website=bleedingcool.com |language=en}}</ref> comic, ''[[Peacemaker Presents: The Vigilante/Eagly Double Feature!]]'' was announced. The comic centered around ''Peacemaker'' characters [[Adrian Chase|Vigilante]] and Eagly in two stories. Gunn was a story consultant on the comic, which was written by [[Rex Ogle]] and [[Tim Seeley]] with art by Matteo Lolli and Mitch Gerads. The comic released monthly from March through August 2025.<ref name="BC2">{{cite web |last1=Johnston |first1=Rich |title=DC Comics Confirms James Gunn On New Peacemaker/Eagly/Vigilante Comic |url=https://bleedingcool.com/comics/dc-comics-confirms-james-gunn-on-new-peacemaker-eagly-vigilante-comic/ |publisher=[[Bleeding Cool]] |access-date=16 September 2025|date=October 19, 2024}}</ref><ref name="G">{{cite web |last1=Carter |first1=Justin |title=DC Came to NYCC With Absolute Reveals and Some Big Returns |date=October 20, 2024 |url=https://gizmodo.com/nycc-dc-comics-absolute-vertigo-2000514145 |publisher=[[Gizmodo]] |access-date=16 September 2025}}</ref>

